## Follow-up review: issue #40 (lock revision)

The shared storage queue fixes the prior disk-write interleaving issue: imports and queued writes no longer execute their filesystem operations at the same time.

### Remaining finding

1. High: queued normal mutations still capture and mutate stale in-memory state *before* they acquire the new lock, so they can overwrite the imported collection after the import completes. For example, [AppState.addSession](/home/harsha/workspace/Citta/citta/lib/providers/app_state.dart:125) immediately assigns `_sessions = [..._sessions, session]` and only then queues `saveSessions`; if an import replacing sessions is in flight, `_sessions` is the pre-import list. Once the import commits its imported sessions, the queued save writes that stale list plus the new session, silently dropping every imported session. [QuoteService.addUserQuote](/home/harsha/workspace/Citta/citta/lib/services/quote_service.dart:72) has the same pattern for `_userQuotes`, and [AppState.updateConfig](/home/harsha/workspace/Citta/citta/lib/providers/app_state.dart:114) queues a full pre-import `ConfigModel`, which can overwrite unrelated imported settings. The existing concurrency test only queues a raw `saveConfig` with a deliberately final value, so it misses this state-snapshot race. Make the complete read/modify/write state transition wait for the import boundary — e.g. reject mutations while importing, or serialize them at the AppState/QuoteService layer and recompute from the post-import state once admitted. Add deterministic AppState and QuoteService tests that start a replace-all import, issue an add-session/add-quote or one-field config edit before it completes, then assert the queued mutation is applied to (rather than replaces) the imported data.

